:title("Go (subset) to LLVM IR compiler") ;
:description("Compiles a Go subset to an LLVM IR module and runs it with the built-in
IR interpreter and the MetaJS handle runtime (llvm.RunJS).

The subset: package and import clauses (parsed, ignored), functions with typed
parameters and multiple return values (func divmod(a int, b int) (int, int)), methods
with value receivers (func (p Point) manhattan() int), struct types with positional
composite literals (Point{3, 4}), field access and assignment, := short declarations
(also multiple: q, r := divmod(7, 2)), var declarations with zero values, parallel
assignment (a, b = b, a) and the blank identifier _, slices ([]int{...}, append, len,
indexing, element assignment), maps (map[K]V{...} literals and make(map[K]V); reads
of missing keys yield the zero value of the value type - int/string/bool -, m[k] = v,
len, delete(m, k), the comma ok idiom v, ok := m[k], and range over the entries),
function literals as values (func(x int) int { ... }) with real closures over their
scope, defer for plain and pkg.fn calls (arguments evaluate at defer time, the calls
run LIFO at function exit), the three for forms (classic, condition only, and
for i, v := range s - also over an int like range 5, Go 1.22, and over maps), switch
with subject
(case 2, 3: compares ==) and without (the first true case wins; no fallthrough, an
implicit break ends every case), Go style if/else without parentheses (else if works), break,
continue, i++ and i-- as statements, assignment operators += -= *= /= %=, fmt.Println
and fmt.Print, os.Exit, and string concatenation. int is 64 bit, / truncates.

The type switch (switch v := x.(type)) and type assertions are runtime type tests.
Goroutines, channels (make, send, receive, close, the comma-ok receive) and select run
on a cooperative scheduler: a goroutine runs when a receive finds its channel empty.

Also implemented (added while widening toward the full language, and measured by
tests/go-test-full.go): grouped declarations - several names sharing a type in
parameters and in struct fields -, multi-name var declarations with a shared type or
an unpacked multi-value initializer, and the full operator set (bitwise and or xor
and-not, shifts, the matching compound assignments, unary complement). A line break
now ends a var spec or a struct field, so a following name is not read as the
previous one's type. Slice expressions (s[lo:hi], byte ranges on a string), the
bracketed conversions []byte(s) / []rune(s), 'for {}', labels with labelled break and
continue and a backward goto, generics ERASED (type parameter lists and instantiations
f[int] / T[K, V] are parsed and ignored), defined types (type myInt int becomes the
conversion to its underlying type), keyed and empty struct literals, Go's own escape
sequences (a byte-wise x and octal escape, and
the eight digit U form), strings as BYTE strings (len and s[i] count bytes, and
ranging one yields byte offsets and runes), the identifier spelled conversions
(string(r), int(x), byte(x), ...), calls on arbitrary values (fs[0]()), variadic
parameters and the f(xs...) spread, named results (a bare return yields them and a
deferred function may still change them), defer on a function literal or a method
value, per iteration loop variables (Go 1.22), an init statement in an if or switch
header, fallthrough, and the min/max/clear builtins.

Deviations and limits: structs behave like references (as if every value were a *T),
append mutates in place (write s = append(s, v) as usual), composite literals must not
appear in if/for/switch headers, map iteration and printing follow the insertion order
(real Go randomizes resp. sorts), and a nil map reads as empty.
There is one numeric type at runtime, except where a written-out
conversion makes the type known while the code is emitted: float64(i)/2 is 2.5 and a
sized unsigned type wraps to its width, so uint8(0xF0) complements to 0x0F. A pointer
to a plain variable IS modelled (and so is new(T) of a scalar): it is a cell object
over the variable's scope slot, so *p = v, f(&n) and **pp = v write through and
p == &n holds. Every other &-form is the identity on the value, since structs, slices
and maps behave like references already.

An ARRAY is a plain runtime array and a SLICE is a $sl header {a, o, n, c} over one,
so the two behave as Go says: an array is a VALUE (assigning it copies, deeply), while
s[1:3] shares the backing array, cap() answers something of its own, s[:2:3] caps the
capacity and append writes into the shared storage while it fits. The handle runtime's
js_golen / js_goslice / js_goappend externs only know plain arrays, so len, cap, index,
slice, append, copy, clear, range and print all go through GENERATED functions that
reach through the header first (see declSlices). complex128 is a $cx {re, im} class
instance, and the four arithmetic operators route through a generated dispatch only in
a program that mentions one.

UNTYPED CONSTANTS are evaluated EXACTLY at compile time and rounded ONCE, where the
value reaches a typed location, which is Go's rule and not an optimisation: 0.1 + 0.2
is the double nearest three tenths (0.3), (1 << 100) >> 98 is 4, 2.0/3.0 !=
0.6666666666666666 even though both round to the same double, and a complex constant
folds as a PAIR of exact rationals. Literals, NAMED constants and expressions over
them all fold; the front end carries its own parse-time scope for the names, and
DECLINES - falling back to ordinary runtime arithmetic - wherever it cannot prove the
name is that constant: a spec with a written-out TYPE (which Go rounds at the
declaration), anything in a const group mentioning iota or repeating the previous
spec's expressions, a name used earlier in the file than its declaration, and a name
that any other binder (a var, :=, a parameter, a receiver, a named result, a range or
type-switch variable, an assignment target, a func, a type, an import alias) owns
where it is used. A written-out conversion is Go's own reason not to fold, so
float64(0.1) + float64(0.2) really is 0.30000000000000004. complex(0.1, 0.2) is a
constant expression in Go and is NOT folded here (the builtin call is not a fold
site), and division or remainder by zero, a shift count over 4096 and an intermediate
wider than 700 limbs each abandon the fold - all four are compile errors or
unrepresentable in real Go anyway.


Functions, methods and function literals become IR functions jsf_N(env, args) (methods
get the receiver
prepended, literals close over their scope via js_closure), struct types become
descriptor objects and composite literals set the fields
positionally (the field lists are known at compile time), method calls dispatch through
js_mcall, multiple return values travel as arrays and are unpacked by multi assignments.
Maps are js_dict_new handles (two parallel key/value arrays keep the insertion order)
with their zero value stored on the map; indexing goes through the polymorphic
js_map_get/js_pyset externals, range loops through js_range_len/key/val (one emission
covers ints, slices and maps), and defer collects [f, args] pairs on a per function
$defers list that js_gorundefers runs LIFO before every return.
The whole program becomes jsmain(env, args), which runs the top level and calls main().
The entry point is func main(); self checking programs end with os.Exit(failures).
The same language with the same semantics is interpreted directly by go-interpreter.abnf.") ;


// --- main rules

// Shared token productions (languages/lib).
:include("lib/cstyle-comments.abnf") ;
:include("lib/ident.abnf") ;
:include("lib/dq-strings.abnf") ;
:include("lib/numbers.abnf") ;
:include("lib/bools.abnf") ;

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;

Program     <~~ buildMain(takeAll()) ~~>
            = PackageClause { Import } { TypeDecl | TypeGroup | ConstGroup | ConstSingle | FuncDecl | TopVar } ;

PackageClause = "package" KwEnd Id [ ";" ] ;
// An import spec may carry a local name: an alias (import f "fmt"), the blank
// identifier (import _ "x", imported for its side effects only) or a dot
// (import . "fmt", which drops the package's exported names into file scope).
// The alias really binds - it is declared in the module scope as a second name
// for the package object - so f.Println works; the dot form is parsed and
// reported not implemented, because file-scope injection has no model here.
Import      <~~ push(makeSeq(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "import" KwEnd ( ImportSpec | "(" { ImportSpec } ")" ) [ ";" ] ;
ImportSpec  = ImportDot | ImportNamed | ImportPlain ;
ImportPlain <~~ push(makeImport(pop(), up.pos)) ~~> = ImportPath [ ";" ] ;
ImportNamed <~~ push(makeImportAs(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(cPoisonName(up.in)) ~~> ImportPath [ ";" ] ;
ImportDot   <~~ push(makeImport(pop(), up.pos)); notImpl("dot import", up.pos) ~~>
            = "." ImportPath [ ";" ] ;
ImportPath  <~~ push(up.in.slice(1, up.in.length - 1)) ~~>
            = '"' :whitespace() { "\x20"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x7e" } '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// type Point struct { x int; y int } and type Shape interface { Area() int }.
// The name is pushed first; the body decides between a struct (lowered) and an
// interface (its method set recorded).
// `type A = B` is an ALIAS: A and B name the same type rather than two distinct ones,
// so the alias is declared as a second name for whatever B is bound to (a struct
// descriptor stays a struct descriptor, which a conversion wrapper would have
// destroyed). The trailing ';' is the pre-1.0 spelling the corpus is full of.
TypeDecl    <~~ push(makeTypeDecl(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "type" KwEnd Id <~~ push(cPoisonName(up.in)) ~~> [ TypeParams ] [ AliasEq ] ( StructBody | IfaceBody | DefinedType ) [ ";" ] ;
AliasEq     <~~ push({alias: true}) ~~> = "=" ;
// type myInt int / type Celsius float64: a defined type over an existing one. The value
// model has no distinct type for it, so the name becomes the CONVERSION to its
// underlying type - which is exactly how a defined type is used in an expression.
DefinedType <~~ push({under: firstType(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Type ;
// Struct fields: one or more names sharing a type (x, y int), an optional raw string
// tag, and embedded fields (a bare type name, whose field name is the type).
StructBody  = "struct" KwEnd "{" { FieldDecl } "}" ;
FieldDecl   = NamedField | EmbedField ;
NamedField  = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } SameLine FType [ FieldTag ] [ ";" ] ;
// The field's type TEXT, which gives the field its zero value in a literal that
// leaves it out (Go zero-fills; an absent property would read back as undefined).
FType       <~~ push({fty: goWalkQual(firstType(up.in))}) ~~> = Type ;
// An embedded field's NAME is its type name (w.base09, plus the promoted w.id); an
// embedded INTERFACE keeps its type, so a literal boxes the value put there.
EmbedField  = [ "*" ] [ Id "." ] Id <~~ push(up.in); push({fty: goIsIfaceTy(up.in) ? up.in : ""}) ~~> [ FieldTag ] [ ";" ] ;
FieldTag    = RawString <~~ pop() ~~> ;
// The method set is parsed when the body is an ordinary method list; any other body
// falls through to SkipBlock with NO method names ("satisfied by anything"). Twin of
// IfaceBody in go-interpreter.abnf; see there.
IfaceBody   <~~ push({iface: true, imeth: cIfaceMeths(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = "interface" KwEnd ( IfaceMethods | SkipBlock ) ;
IfaceMethods = "{" { IfaceMeth } "}" ;
IfaceMeth   = Id <~~ push({imname: up.in}) ~~> FTParams [ SameLine FTRets ] [ ";" ] ;

// A grouped type declaration (type ( A struct {...}; B int )). The whole body is
// balanced-skipped and reported not implemented: unlike a single struct decl, its
// members are not lowered. Tried after TypeDecl (which needs an Id, not '(').
TypeGroup   <~~ push(notImplStmt("grouped type declaration", up.pos)) ~~>
            = "type" KwEnd SkipGroup [ ";" ] ;

// const Name [Type] = expr is GENUINELY bound as if it were a var (its value is
// reused via the var-decl thunk). A grouped const block binds its names too: `iota`
// is the index of the spec inside the group, and a spec with no '=' REPEATS the last
// expression list seen, re-emitted with the new iota (which is how red/green/blue and
// 1 << (10 * iota) count up).
ConstGroup  <~~ push(makeConstGroup(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "const" KwEnd CGrpOn "(" { ConstSpec } ")" [ ";" ] ;
ConstSpec   <~~ push(makeConstSpec(cBindSpec(takeAll(), up.start))) ~~>
            = CSpecOn Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> } [ SameLine VType ] [ "=" CExprList ] [ ";" ] ;
// An ungrouped const spec BINDS SEVERAL NAMES (`const a, b = 0.1, 0.2`), like the
// grouped ConstSpec above and the interpreter half's ConstSingle. makeVarDecl handles
// n names and n initializers (it is the builder `var a, b = 1, 2` uses).
ConstSingle <~~ push(makeVarDecl(cBindSpec(takeAll(), up.start))) ~~>
            = "const" KwEnd CGrpOn CSpecOn Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> }
              [ VType ] [ "=" CExprList ] [ ";" ] ;
// The spec index inside the const group, which is what `iota` means.
CSpecOn     <~~ cIotaIdx = cIotaIdx + 1 ~~> = [ "\x00" ] ;
// The group watermark: `iota` is the index of the spec INSIDE this const block, so the
// counter and the repeated expression list are reset at every `const`. Grouped and
// ungrouped both carry it, which is what makes a lone `const c = iota` zero.
CGrpOn      <~~ cIotaIdx = 0 - 1; cLastFns = [] ~~> = [ "\x00" ] ;
// An expression list that also tags each element's END POSITION - the key its folded
// constant was registered under, and the only way the binding can read it back.
CExprList   = CExprItem { "," CExprItem } ;
CExprItem   <~~ push({cat: up.pos, cks: up.start}) ~~> = Expression ;

// func name(params) rets { } and func (r Type) name(params) rets { }
// The result list is optional, so like FuncTypeT's it has to be pinned to the line the
// parameters ended on: Go inserts a semicolon there, and `func (t T) m()` followed by
// `func main() {` otherwise reads the next declaration's `func` as this one's result
// type (NamedType's Id happily matches the keyword).
// A function may be declared WITHOUT a body: its implementation comes from assembly
// (or from another file the linker supplies), which this project can never provide.
// The syntax is accepted so the rest of the file parses; the missing body is reported
// as not implemented, so nobody mistakes the acceptance for support. The bodied form
// is tried first, so `func f() {` never reaches here.
FuncDecl    = FuncBodied | FuncNoBody ;
FuncBodied  <~~ push(makeFuncStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "func" KwEnd [ Receiver ] Id <~~ push(cPoisonName(up.in)) ~~> [ TypeParams ] ParamList [ SameLine RetTypes ] FuncBody [ ";" ] ;
FuncNoBody  <~~ push(makeFuncStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "func" KwEnd [ Receiver ] Id <~~ push(cPoisonName(up.in)) ~~> [ TypeParams ] ParamList [ SameLine RetTypes ] NoBody [ ";" ] ;
// An always-empty match: Go source holds no NUL byte, so the optional never consumes
// anything and the production stands purely for its tag.
NoBody      <~~ push(notImplStmt("function without a body", up.pos)) ~~> = [ "\x00" ] ;
// Generics are ERASED: a type parameter list is parsed and ignored, the constraint with
// it, and an instantiation (f[int], T[K, V]) is the identity on the value it indexes.
// The value model is dynamically typed, so an erased generic behaves exactly like the
// instantiated one - only compile-time type CHECKING is lost, which a syntax-driven
// front end does not do anyway.
TypeParams  = "[" TypeParamGrp { "," TypeParamGrp } "]" ;
TypeParamGrp = Id <~~ goNoteTParam(up.in) ~~> { "," Id <~~ goNoteTParam(up.in) ~~> } Constraint ;
Constraint  = ConstrTerm { "|" ConstrTerm } ;
ConstrTerm  = [ "~" ] Type ;
// A POINTER receiver is marked: its method sees the caller's struct, while a value
// receiver is handed a copy (js_gomcall / js_gofield read the __ptr$name marker).
// A method may also leave the receiver UNNAMED (func (T) M(), func (*T) M()) when the
// body does not use it - very common in interface-satisfaction tests. Which shape it is
// is decided by a lookahead rather than by letting the named form try and fail, because
// `(T)` matches the named form's first Id and only dies at the second one. RecvAnon
// pushes an {anon} marker LAST (a tag runs after its children), and normRecv turns that
// into the canonical [name, {byptr}?, type] the method lowering expects, with an empty
// name.
Receiver    <~~ push({recv: normRecv(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = "(" ( RecvNamed | RecvAnon ) ")" ;
RecvNamed   = RecvNamedAhead Id <~~ push(cPendName(up.in)) ~~> [ "*" <~~ push({byptr: true}) ~~> ] Id <~~ push(cPendName(up.in)) ~~> [ TypeArgs ] ;
RecvAnon    <~~ push({anon: true}) ~~>
            = [ "*" <~~ push({byptr: true}) ~~> ] Id <~~ push(cPendName(up.in)) ~~> [ TypeArgs ] ;
// The receiver is NAMED exactly when an identifier is followed by another identifier
// or by '*' - `(p *T)` and `(p T)` are named, `(T)`, `(*T)` and `(T[P])` are not.
// A :script SUCCEEDS by returning undefined and FAILS by returning an impossible
// token, so the named case is the `undefined` one.
RecvNamedAhead = :script(~~
    (function () {
        var i = 0
        var cc = 0
        while (i < 256) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { i++; continue }
            break
        }
        if (cc < 0) { return abnf.newToken("\x01anon-receiver", 0) }
        var isWord = (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || cc === 95 || cc >= 128
        if (!isWord) { return abnf.newToken("\x01anon-receiver", 0) }
        while (i < 512) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if ((cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) || (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || cc === 95 || cc >= 128) { i++; continue }
            break
        }
        while (i < 768) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { i++; continue }
            break
        }
        if (cc < 0) { return abnf.newToken("\x01anon-receiver", 0) }
        if (cc === 42) { return undefined }
        if ((cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || cc === 95 || cc >= 128) { return undefined }
        return abnf.newToken("\x01anon-receiver", 0)
    })()
~~) ;
// Parameters: several names may share a type (a, b int), a name may be omitted
// entirely (func(int), only in type positions), and the last one may be variadic.
// A named group is tried first; on an unnamed list it fails and the bare-type form
// takes over.
ParamList   <~~ push(makeParams(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "(" [ ParamItem { "," ParamItem } [ "," ] ] ")" ;
ParamItem   = NamedParam | AnonParam ;
NamedParam  = Id <~~ push(cPendName(up.in)) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(cPendName(up.in)) ~~> } [ Variadic ] PType ;
AnonParam   = [ Variadic ] PType ;
// The parameter's declared TYPE TEXT, captured the way FType captures a struct
// field's. A parameter is an ordinary slot, so `func f(b uint8)` has to put the
// width on the argument on the way in - without this the b++ inside f stepped at
// 53 bits.
PType       <~~ push({pty: goWalkQual(firstType(up.in))}) ~~> = Type ;
// The last parameter may be variadic: it collects the remaining arguments as a slice.
Variadic    <~~ push({vararg: true}) ~~> = "..." ;
// f(xs...) spreads a slice into a variadic parameter instead of passing it as one
// argument.
Spread      <~~ push({spread: true}) ~~> = "..." ;
// Results: a single type, or a parenthesized list that may NAME its results
// (func f(a int) (q, r int)). Named results become zero-valued locals that a bare
// 'return' yields and that a deferred function can still change. NamedAhead decides
// which shape it is BEFORE any name is pushed, so the unnamed list never leaks half a
// name list onto the tag stack.
// The result type texts are captured, so a named result starts at the zero of its
// own type and a function whose panic was RECOVERED returns the zero values of its
// declared result types - exactly as a PARAMETER carries its type text through PType.
RetTypes    = NamedRets | RetOne | RetList ;
RetOne      <~~ push({rtys: cRty1(up.in)}) ~~> = Type ;
RetList     <~~ push({rtys: cRtyList(takeAll())}) ~~> = "(" [ RType { "," RType } ] ")" ;
RType       <~~ push({rty: firstType(up.in)}) ~~> = Type ;
NamedRets   <~~ push(cNamedRets(takeAll())) ~~>
            = NamedAhead "(" NamedRetGrp { "," NamedRetGrp } ")" ;
NamedRetGrp = Id <~~ push(cPendName(up.in)) ~~> { "," Id <~~ push(cPendName(up.in)) ~~> } RType ;
// A parenthesized list is NAMED exactly when some element at depth 1 is two words
// separated by whitespace ('q, r int' has 'r int'; '(int, string)' has no such pair).
NamedAhead = :script(~~
    (function () {
        var i = 0
        var depth = 0
        var cc = 0
        var prevWord = false
        var sawSpace = false
        while (i < 8192) {
            cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc < 0) { break }
            var isWord = (cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) || (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || cc === 95
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { sawSpace = true; i++; continue }
            if (cc === 40) { depth++; prevWord = false; sawSpace = false; i++; continue }
            if (cc === 41) {
                depth--
                if (depth === 0) { break }
                prevWord = false
                sawSpace = false
                i++
                continue
            }
            if (depth === 1 && prevWord && sawSpace && (isWord || cc === 91 || cc === 42)) { return undefined }
            prevWord = isWord
            sawSpace = false
            i++
        }
        return abnf.newToken("\x01unnamed-results", 0)
    })()
~~) ;

// A package level var: its declaration thunk is emitted after every other top level
// declaration, in Go's initialisation order (goTopOrder), each spec of a group
// (var ( a = b; b = 2 )) on its own. Twin of TopVar in go-interpreter.abnf.
TopVar      = TopVarGroup | TopVarOne ;
TopVarOne   <~~ push(goTopVarC(pop(), c.srcSlice(up.start, up.pos))) ~~>
            = VarDecl ;
TopVarGroup = "var" KwEnd "(" { TopVarSpec } ")" [ ";" ] ;
TopVarSpec  <~~ push(goTopVarC(pop(), "var " + c.srcSlice(up.start, up.pos))) ~~>
            = VarSpec ;

Type        = "*" Type | "[" "]" Type | ArrayTypeT | MapTypeT | FuncTypeT | ChanTypeT | IfaceTypeT
            | StructTypeT | ( "int" KwEnd | "string" KwEnd | "bool" KwEnd | Id ) [ "." Id ] [ TypeArgs ] ;
// An anonymous struct type in a TYPE position (var x struct{ a int }): parsed, its
// body balanced-skipped. Tried before the bare-name form, which would otherwise read
// 'struct' as an identifier and stop there.
StructTypeT = "struct" KwEnd SkipBlock ;
TypeArgs    = "[" Type { "," Type } "]" ;
// A pointer type (*T) and a fixed size array ([N]T / [...]T) are parsed in every type
// position; the pointer is transparent (structs already behave like references) and an
// array declares like a slice. The size expression is parsed and ignored.
ArrayTypeT  = "[" ( "..." | Expression <~~ pop() ~~> ) "]" Type ;
MapTypeT    = "map" KwEnd "[" Type "]" Type ;
// A func TYPE may name its parameters and its results exactly like a declaration does
// (type Seq[V any] func(yield func(V) bool)); the names are parsed and dropped, since a
// type carries no bindings. The shape mirrors ParamList/RetTypes, untagged - a Type has
// to stay item-neutral, so none of these productions may push.
// The result is optional, so it must not be allowed to run over the line end: Go
// inserts a semicolon after the ')' that ends the parameters when the line stops
// there, and without SameLine the optional result happily reads the NEXT top-level
// `func` declaration as this type's return type.
FuncTypeT   = "func" KwEnd FTParams [ SameLine FTRets ] ;
FTParams    = "(" [ FTItem { "," FTItem } [ "," ] ] ")" ;
FTItem      = FTNamed | FTAnon ;
FTNamed     = Id { "," Id } [ "..." ] Type ;
FTAnon      = [ "..." ] Type ;
FTRets      = FTRetNamed | Type | "(" [ Type { "," Type } ] ")" ;
FTRetNamed  = NamedAhead "(" FTRetGrp { "," FTRetGrp } ")" ;
FTRetGrp    = Id { "," Id } Type ;
// Channel and (anonymous) interface types are parsed in every type position.
ChanTypeT   = ( "chan" KwEnd [ "<-" ] | "<-" "chan" KwEnd ) Type ;
IfaceTypeT  = "interface" KwEnd SkipBlock ;

// Every statement production pushes exactly one thunk: function() -> undefined | signal.
Statement   <~~ push(stmtPos(pop())) ~~>
            = Block | If | For | Switch | Return | Break | Continue | Goto | Defer | GoStmt | Select | Fallthrough
            | TypeDecl | TypeGroup | ConstGroup | ConstSingle | VarGroup | VarDecl | Labeled | SimpleStmt | Empty ;

// fallthrough: continues into the NEXT case body instead of leaving the switch.
Fallthrough <~~ push({isFallthrough: true}) ~~>
            = "fallthrough" KwEnd [ ";" ] ;

// go f(args) / go pkg.Fn(args): a goroutine. Concurrency is COOPERATIVE and
// deterministic (the model of go-interpreter.abnf, shared through the runtime): the
// call is emitted into a closure that js_gospawn only QUEUES, and a receive that
// finds its channel empty runs the queue until one of them fills it.
GoStmt      <~~ push(makeGoStmt(pop(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "go" KwEnd PostfixExpr [ ";" ] ;
// select { case v := <-ch: ...; case ch <- x: ...; default: ... }: the arms are tried
// in source order and the first READY one runs. An arm body is a statement list, so
// NotSelLabel stops it before the next 'case' / 'default' (both would otherwise parse
// as an identifier expression).
Select      <~~ push(makeSelect(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "select" KwEnd "{" { SelArm } "}" ;
SelArm      = SelRecv | SelSend | SelDefault ;
SelRecv     <~~ push(makeSelRecv(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "case" KwEnd [ Id <~~ push(cPoisonName(up.in)) ~~> [ "," Id <~~ push(cPoisonName(up.in)) ~~> ] ( ":=" | "=" ) ]
              "<-" Expression ":" SelBody ;
SelSend     <~~ push(makeSelSend(takeAll())) ~~>
